From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Subject: [PATCH 0019] drm_hwcomposer: stop destroying the display while reporting a hotplug

DrmHwcThree::HandleDisplayHotplugEvent() erased the HwcDisplay object out of
DrmHwc::displays_ while merely *reporting* a hotplug. Those objects are owned by DrmHwc
and their lifetime belongs to BindDisplay()/UnbindDisplay()/FinalizeDisplayBinding();
erasing one here destroys it behind DrmHwc's back. display_handles_ still maps the
pipeline to handle 0 while displays_ no longer holds it, so GetDisplay() returns null and
every subsequent HWC3 call answers BAD_DISPLAY (2).

It ran on every detach AND on every attach after the first, because
HwcDisplay::SetPipeline() always schedules kConnected for the primary display
(SurfaceFlinger LOG_ALWAYS_FATALs if the primary is reported disconnected), while
AddPhysicalDisplay() is called only from this function - so once display 0 has been
announced, HasDisplay(0) stays true forever.

On this board the result was that an HDMI sink going into standby, or being attached
after a sink-less boot, left SurfaceFlinger with a dead display 0 ("Failed to hotplug
display 0") and a permanent "Invalid display" error storm until reboot; see
docs/evidence/2026-09-18/display-hwc-storm.txt, about 1200 errors per second.

The fix keeps the buffer-cache bookkeeping (RemoveDisplay/AddPhysicalDisplay) and drops
the erase. Upstream drm_hwcomposer has removed this whole block, which is corroboration.
CleanDisplayResources() is deliberately no longer called on the re-announcement path: its
first act is SetPowerMode(OFF), which - with the display now surviving - would really
execute and DPMS off the pipeline SetPipeline() has just built; its cache work is
redundant because SetPipeline()->Deinit() has already released the client target and
RemoveDisplay() drops the cache. The function is left defined but unused, so the diff
stays minimal.

Nothing about composition strategy is touched, so patch 0009's YUV overlay and
full-screen RGB client target are unaffected. No logging suppression, no unconditional
success return, no composer restart.

Built into vendor/apex/com.android.hardware.graphics.composer.drm_hwcomposer.apex
(sha256 4d65af97..., was 50acec62...); the only file that differs inside the APEX payload
is bin/hw/android.hardware.composer.hwc3-service.drm.
---
diff --git a/external/drm_hwcomposer/hwc3/DrmHwcThree.cpp b/external/drm_hwcomposer/hwc3/DrmHwcThree.cpp
--- a/external/drm_hwcomposer/hwc3/DrmHwcThree.cpp
+++ b/external/drm_hwcomposer/hwc3/DrmHwcThree.cpp
@@ -143,17 +143,39 @@
 void DrmHwcThree::HandleDisplayHotplugEvent(uint64_t display_id,
                                             bool connected) {
   DEBUG_FUNC();
+  /* h96 21.09: do NOT erase from Displays() here.
+   *
+   * The HwcDisplay objects are owned by DrmHwc; their lifetime belongs to
+   * BindDisplay()/UnbindDisplay()/FinalizeDisplayBinding(). Erasing one while
+   * merely *reporting* a hotplug destroys it behind DrmHwc's back:
+   * display_handles_ still maps the pipeline to handle 0 while displays_ no
+   * longer holds it, so GetDisplay() returns null and every subsequent HWC3
+   * call answers BAD_DISPLAY.
+   *
+   * This ran on every detach AND on every attach after the first, because
+   * HwcDisplay::SetPipeline() always schedules kConnected for the primary
+   * display (SurfaceFlinger aborts if the primary is reported disconnected),
+   * while AddPhysicalDisplay() is called only from here -- so once display 0
+   * has been announced, HasDisplay(0) is true forever. The result on this
+   * board was that an HDMI sink going into standby, or being attached after a
+   * sink-less boot, left SurfaceFlinger with a dead display and a permanent
+   * "Invalid display" error storm until reboot.
+   *
+   * Upstream drm_hwcomposer has removed this block entirely. Keep only the
+   * buffer-cache bookkeeping. */
   if (!connected) {
     composer_resources_->RemoveDisplay(display_id);
-    Displays().erase(display_id);
     return;
   }
 
   if (composer_resources_->HasDisplay(display_id)) {
-    /* Cleanup existing display resources */
-    CleanDisplayResources(display_id);
+    /* Re-announcement of a display we already advertised (pipeline swap, or a
+     * headless <-> real transition). SetPipeline()->Deinit() has already
+     * released the client target, so dropping the stale buffer cache is all
+     * that is needed. CleanDisplayResources() is deliberately NOT called: it
+     * begins with SetPowerMode(OFF), which would now really execute and DPMS
+     * off the pipeline that SetPipeline() has just built. */
     composer_resources_->RemoveDisplay(display_id);
-    Displays().erase(display_id);
   }
   composer_resources_->AddPhysicalDisplay(display_id);
 }
